          "content": "You are an illustrator who writes zpup scenes by hand. The zpup reference is below.\n\nDraw the requested subject as one zpup scene in JSON, 400 by 400 pixels.\nMake it look three-dimensional: give the parts volume and depth, and make it clear which parts are in front of others.\nUse only what zpup itself provides. Write plain JSON, not TypeScript or JavaScript.\nReply with the JSON scene only: no explanation and no Markdown code fence.\n\n<reference>\n# zpup\n\n> zpup renders round, flat pseudo-3D illustrations (in the style of Zdog) from a plain JSON scene tree to an SVG string. ESM-only TypeScript, no runtime dependencies, no DOM needed.\n\n## Entry points\n\nThe npm package is `@zpup/core`.\n\n```ts\nimport { renderSvg, renderSpinSvg, checkScene, compileScene, ZpupValidationError } from '@zpup/core';\nimport { group, path, dot, ellipse, rect, polygon, box, cylinder, cone, hemisphere } from '@zpup/core'; // optional typed helpers\n\nrenderSvg(scene, { precision: 2 }) // -> \"<svg ...>...</svg>\\n\" (pure, deterministic)\ncheckScene(scene)                  // -> ValidationIssue[] (empty when valid), never throws\ncheckSceneWarnings(scene)          // -> ValidationIssue[]: valid but suspicious (radians, off-screen, draws nothing)\ncompileScene(scene)                // -> Frame: projected, depth-sorted 2D draw items\nrenderSpinSvg(scene, options?)     // -> \"<svg ...>\" that turns the scene forever (SMIL, no JavaScript)\n```\n\n`renderSvg`, `renderSpinSvg` and `compileScene` throw `ZpupValidationError` on invalid input. Its `message` lists every problem; `issues` has `{ path, expected, received, hint, message }` for each.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- Axes: x right, y down, z toward the viewer. Origin = center of the image.\n- Units: scene units; 1 unit = 1 SVG pixel at `zoom: 1`.\n- Angles: degrees, never radians. Rotation applies z first, then y, then x.\n  - `rotate.z > 0`: clockwise on screen.\n  - `rotate.y > 0`: the +x side moves away from the viewer.\n  - `rotate.x > 0`: the bottom (+y) side moves toward the viewer. `scene.rotate = { x: -15 }` looks down from above.\n- Vectors are objects with optional keys: `{ x: 10 }`, `{ y: -5, z: 2 }`, `{}`. Arrays such as `[1, 2, 3]` are rejected.\n- Node transform order: scale, then rotate, then translate. Children inherit the parent's transform.\n- Projection is orthographic. There is no perspective and no lighting.\n- Unknown options are errors (this catches typos such as `colour`).\n\n## Scene\n\n```ts\n{\n  width: number,          // required, pixels, > 0\n  height: number,         // required, pixels, > 0\n  zoom?: number,          // default 1\n  background?: string,    // CSS color; default transparent\n  title?: string,         // accessible title\n  rotate?: { x?, y?, z? },// view rotation in degrees\n  children: Node[],       // required (may be [])\n}\n```\n\n## Nodes\n\nEvery node: `type` (required), `id?` (string, shown in errors), `translate?`, `rotate?`, `scale?` (number or `{ x?, y?, z? }`, missing axes = 1), `visible?` (default true), `children?`.\n\nEvery shape (all types except `group`): `color?` (CSS color, default `\"#333\"`), `stroke?` (width >= 0, default 1, round caps and joins), `fill?` (boolean, default false; fills with `color`).\n\n| type | own options | notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `group` | none | Draws nothing; moves its children together. No `color`. |\n| `path` | `path` (required, array of points), `closed?` (default false) | 1 point = dot/sphere, 2 = line/tube, 3+ = polyline or polygon. |\n| `ellipse` | `diameter`, or `width` and `height` | Circle or ellipse in the local x/y plane. |\n| `rect` | `width`, `height` (required) | Centered on the origin, in the local x/y plane. |\n| `polygon` | `sides` (integer >= 3), `radius` (required) | Regular polygon, one corner pointing up. |\n| `box` | `width`, `height`, `depth` (required); `frontFace`, `rearFace`, `leftFace`, `rightFace`, `topFace`, `bottomFace` | Centered. Faces: front +z, top -y. |\n| `cylinder` | `diameter`, `length` (required); `frontFace` (+z end), `backface` (-z end) | Along local z, centered. |\n| `cone` | `diameter`, `length` (required); `backface` (base) | Base at z = 0, tip at z = length. |\n| `hemisphere` | `diameter` (required); `backface` (flat side) | Flat side at z = 0, dome toward +z. |\n\nSolids (`box`, `cylinder`, `cone`, `hemisphere`) are always filled and take `color`, `stroke` (default 1, rounds the edges) and `shade?` (default true). Each face gets a fixed lighter or darker version of `color` by the direction it points in the scene (up lighter, down darker, left and right different), so one color reads as 3D. Face options set one face's exact color. `shade: false` paints every face in `color`. Like Zdog, round solids point along z: `rotate: { x: 90 }` stands them up (the +z end, `frontFace`, goes on top).\n\nPath entries are points `{ x?, y?, z? }` or cubic curves `{ \"bezier\": [control1, control2, end] }`. The first entry must be a point.\n\nHelpers: `group(o)`, `path(o)`, `ellipse(o)`, `rect(o)`, `polygon(o)` return `{ ...o, type }`. `dot(o)` returns a `path` with `path: [{}]`.\n\n## How to get volume\n\n- Sphere: `dot({ stroke: 40 })` (diameter = stroke).\n- Tube or limb: a 2-point `path` with a thick `stroke`.\n- Disc or plate: `ellipse({ diameter, fill: true })`, optionally with a stroke for a rounded rim.\n- Ring: `ellipse({ diameter, stroke })` without fill. Rotate it with `rotate: { x: 90 }` to lay it flat.\n- Lay a flat shape on the ground with `rotate: { x: 90 }`.\n- Mug: `{ \"type\": \"cylinder\", \"diameter\": 80, \"length\": 90, \"rotate\": { \"x\": 90 }, \"color\": \"#4a7bd0\", \"frontFace\": \"#5b3a29\" }` (coffee on top), plus a thick `path` for the handle.\n- Roof or rocket nose: a `cone` with `rotate: { x: 90 }`, placed on the top of a cylinder.\n- Dome or rock: `hemisphere` with `rotate: { x: 90 }`; `scale: { z: 0.5 }` flattens it.\n- Crate, building, robot parts: `box`. Stack solids so they touch on flat faces.\n\n## Depth sorting\n\nShapes are painted back to front. Overlapping shapes are compared where they overlap on screen, not by their centers, so a dot standing on a large floor sorts correctly. Unfilled outlines are sorted segment by segment, so a ring can pass behind and in front of a sphere. Shapes that truly intersect each other, or filled shapes that weave through each other, cannot be drawn correctly; split them into smaller shapes. Do not sink one solid into another: rest it on a flat face (a tower on a base's top, not inside a dome). Coplanar shapes keep tree order (later children paint on top).\n\nPlace details slightly in front of the surface they sit on: an eye on a sphere of stroke 40 goes at `z` around 18 to 20.\n\n## Turning animation\n\n`renderSpinSvg(scene, options)` returns one SVG document that turns the view a full circle, forever, with SMIL animation and no JavaScript. It plays inside `<img>` and GitHub READMEs. Without SMIL support the first frame shows, identical to `renderSvg(scene)`.\n\n```ts\n{\n  axis?: 'x' | 'y' | 'z',              // default 'y': adds the turning angle to scene.rotate.y (a turntable)\n  duration?: number,                   // seconds per turn, >= 0.1, default 8\n  keyframes?: number,                  // integer 4..720, default 36; more = smoother and larger\n  direction?: 'positive' | 'negative', // default 'positive': the angle grows\n  precision?: number,                  // integer 0..10, default 1\n}\n```\n\nUnknown or invalid options throw `ZpupValidationError` with paths such as `options.duration`. Only a constant turn about one axis is possible; nothing else in the scene moves. Keep scenes small (tens of shapes): file size grows with the number of pieces and of overlapping pairs. Use opaque colors; translucent outlines show seams.\n\n## Examples\n\nSphere with a ring:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"width\": 120,\n  \"height\": 120,\n  \"rotate\": { \"x\": -20, \"y\": 30 },\n  \"children\": [\n    { \"type\": \"path\", \"path\": [{}], \"stroke\": 40, \"color\": \"#e62\" },\n    { \"type\": \"ellipse\", \"diameter\": 70, \"stroke\": 5, \"color\": \"#636\", \"rotate\": { \"x\": 90 } }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nFace with grouped features:\n\n```ts\nimport { dot, group, path, renderSvg } from '@zpup/core';\n\nconst svg = renderSvg({\n  width: 160,\n  height: 160,\n  rotate: { y: -25 },\n  children: [\n    group({\n      id: 'head',\n      children: [\n        dot({ stroke: 90, color: '#f4c95d' }),\n        dot({ translate: { x: -16, y: -8, z: 40 }, stroke: 10, color: '#222' }),\n        dot({ translate: { x: 16, y: -8, z: 40 }, stroke: 10, color: '#222' }),\n        path({\n          id: 'smile',\n          translate: { y: 12, z: 42 },\n          path: [{ x: -14 }, { bezier: [{ x: -7, y: 10 }, { x: 7, y: 10 }, { x: 14 }] }],\n          stroke: 5,\n          color: '#222',\n        }),\n      ],\n    }),\n  ],\n});\n```\n\nA longer example lives in `examples/pup.ts`.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Radians (`1.57`, `Math.PI / 4`) -> angles are degrees (`90`, `45`).\n- Origin at the top-left as in SVG -> (0, 0) is the center; x and y run from -width/2 to width/2. Negative `y` is up.\n- `fill: \"#fff\"` -> `fill` is true or false; the color goes in `color`: `\"color\": \"#fff\", \"fill\": true`.\n- `stroke: \"#000\"` or `\"12px\"` -> `stroke` is a width in scene units (`2`); its color is `color`.\n- `type: \"circle\"` -> `\"ellipse\"` with `diameter`. `\"line\"` -> `\"path\"` with two points. `\"sphere\"` -> a dot (`path` `[{}]` with a thick stroke). `\"cube\"` -> `\"box\"`.\n- `radius` on an ellipse or round solid -> `diameter`. `height` on a cylinder or cone -> `length`.\n- `color` on a `group` -> set it on the shapes inside.\n\n## Not available yet\n\nCanvas rendering, animation other than `renderSpinSvg`'s constant turn, perspective, React or other framework bindings.\n</reference>"
